es of $h$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & ={r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r} & =(2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-94999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bae007ee4068299a4b4b005747985ad13bb9cb305ddf87d91a526afdb610a0a1"} {"question" "q-94999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-94999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-94999" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $g(x,y)={y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂g}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =0\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bae007ee4068299a4b4b005747985ad13bb9cb305ddf87d91a526afdb610a0a1"} {"question" "q-95113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "{r}^{2}(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )"] ["math" "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $w(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2r({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bae007ee4068299a4b4b005747985ad13bb9cb305ddf87d91a526afdb610a0a1"} {"question" "q-95109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2r"] ["math" "2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{r}"] ["math" "\\frac{r}{2}"] ["math" "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95109" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{r}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-165282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1),$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-165282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂y}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-165282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bae007ee4068299a4b4b005747985ad13bb9cb305ddf87d91a526afdb610a0a1"} {"question" "q-165282" "field" "selection" "attribu