Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=8{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bae007ee4068299a4b4b005747985ad13bb9cb305ddf87d91a526afdb610a0a1"} {"question" "q-95174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "8z"] ["math" "8z+{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "8z+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "8{r}^{2}z{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "q-95174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/state.json" "file_sha256" "d7f9877dfa86a81e0f4c430cd615198432b893c2557401c42e9afcea0b48635d"} {"question" "e-8294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/example-8294.json" "file_sha256" "3a50c83f0afff0950bdcd08bbd68736cc29b2b5376a3c06abef2f99e3015c7b5"} {"question" "e-8294" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂f}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/example-8294.json" "file_sha256" "3a50c83f0afff0950bdcd08bbd68736cc29b2b5376a3c06abef2f99e3015c7b5"} {"question" "e-8295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta,$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/example-8295.json" "file_sha256" "ef06c25a3a86008ceff773d30052b770a2005a21a5699458612ca84c59332e2f"} {"question" "e-8295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/example-8295.json" "file_sha256" "ef06c25a3a86008ceff773d30052b770a2005a21a5699458612ca84c59332e2f"} {"question" "e-8296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2),$ find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/example-8296.json" "file_sha256" "669da724d2288a0127e9d0f198bc761501e16492569360dfc7e97d278dcd6f34"} {"question" "e-8296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=2x{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(2r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(4r{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}{z}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070474/example-8296.json" "file_sha256" "669da724d2288a0127e9d0f198bc761501e16492569360dfc7e97d278dcd6f34"}] "usage" {"q-95047" [] "q-94770" [] "e-8294" [] "e-8296" [] "e-8295" []} "basis" 2486 "retractions" [[17592186125159 :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} + 2yz$, find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta }$, where $(x, y, z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, \\theta , z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates."] [17592186125159 :question/worked-solution "In cylindrical coordinates, $x^2+y^2=r^2$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$, so $$f=r^2+2rz\\sin\\theta.$$ Hold $r$ and $z$ fixed while differentiating with respect to $\\theta$: $$f_\\theta=2rz\\cos\\theta.$$"] [17592186125159 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186125164 :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y, z) = xy + y^{2}z + xz^{2}$, find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}$, where $(x, y, z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, \\theta , z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates."] [17592186125164 :question/worked-solution "Substitution gives $$f=r^2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta\\,z+r\\cos\\theta\\,z^2.$$ Holding $r$ and $\\theta$ fixed, differentiate each term with respect to $z$ to get $$f_z=r^2\\sin^2\\theta+2rz\\cos\\theta.$$"] [17592186124323 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates."] [17592186124323 :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta$, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta } &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta } \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}r\\sin \\theta  + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}r\\cos \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = x, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= r\\cos \\theta , \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= 2r\\sin \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta } &= - (r\\cos \\theta )r\\sin \\theta  + (2r\\sin \\theta )r\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&=-r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  + 2r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}r^{2}\\sin (2\\theta )\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124324 :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates."] [17592186124324 :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial r} = \\cos 3\\theta , \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } =-3r\\sin 3\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} &= (\\cos 3\\theta )\\cos \\theta  - \\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\sin 3\\theta )\\sin \\theta  \\\\\n&= \\cos \\theta \\cos 3\\theta  + 3\\sin \\theta \\sin 3\\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124325 :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2)$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}$, where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates."] [17592186124325 :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta$, and $z$, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nand $z=z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } &= \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\theta } \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}r\\sin \\theta  + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}r\\cos \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = 2xz^{3}, \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} = 4yz^{3}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta$, and $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} &= 2rz^{3}\\cos \\theta , \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} &= 4rz^{3}\\sin \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } &= - (2rz^{3}\\cos \\theta )r\\sin \\theta  + (4rz^{3}\\sin \\theta )r\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&=-2r^{2}z^{3}\\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta  + 4r^{2}z^{3}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= 2r^{2}z^{3}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= r^{2}z^{3}\\sin 2\\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123977 17592186123978 17592186123979 17592186123980 17592186123981 17592186124007 17592186124008 17592186124009 17592186124010 17592186124011] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123976 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123976 :answer-field/id] [17592186123976 :answer-field/key] [17592186123976 :answer-field/type] [17592186124006 :answer-field/correct] [17592186124006 :answer-field/id] [17592186124006 :answer-field/key] [17592186124006 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
